#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""pm-mini-probe — a read-only, allowlisted stdio MCP server that runs one
``ssh <pod> <cmd>`` per call so the PM session can take its own measurements
on the pod instead of asking the operator to paste terminal output.

Brief: internal/dispatch/done/pm-mini-probe-readonly-mcp.md
Charter: internal/PM-CHARTER.md § Access.

Why this exists
---------------
The PM's cloud sandbox is HTTPS-only and the device bridge's shell is an
isolated VM with no network and no SSH keys, so every pod measurement — the
turns-log forensics, ``context_census.py``, the interpreter check,
``ensure-pod-perms --check-only`` after a merge — was a "run this and paste"
round trip through the operator's terminal. The one door that exists is the
desktop app's proxy for **locally installed MCP servers**: a stdio server on
the operator's laptop that wraps ``ssh`` appears to the PM session as a
callable tool.

The scope the operator ratified 2026-09-05, and what this file implements:
**read-only by construction, allowlisted command shapes, sudo only for the
specific reads that need it, every call logged, unregisterable in one step.**

The privileged half is one root-owned, fixed-argv reader
(``/usr/local/libexec/pm-probe-cat``) plus a short list of exact
``evolve-admin`` read forms. ``sudo cat <path>`` is REWRITTEN to that reader
before it is sent — sudo's own wildcards cannot bound a path operand (they
cross ``/`` and spaces), and ``cat`` follows symlinks out of the bot-writable
trees this door reads. See ``packages/admin/evolve_admin/pm_probe_cat.py``.

Shape of the guarantee
----------------------
* ONE tool, ``run(cmd, timeout_s=60)``. No file transfer, no interactive
  session, no second host.
* The allowlist is **deny-by-default**: a command runs only if it matches one
  of the four named allow rules below. Everything else is refused, and the
  refusal names the rule that caught it so the PM can see *why* rather than
  guessing.
* Named deny rules run FIRST, so the dangerous shapes (metacharacters, ``..``
  traversal, an ``openclaw`` invocation, a mutating binary, a non-``print``
  ``launchctl``, a mutating ``evolve-admin`` or ``git`` verb, a write flag)
  are refused by name instead of by the generic "not allowlisted".
* Shell metacharacters are refused outright — **one command per call**. There
  is no chaining, no substitution, no redirection, therefore no way to
  assemble a write out of read primitives.
* Credential-shaped operands (``auth*``, ``credentials*``, ``.env*``,
  ``*.pem``, ``*.key``, ``id_*``, anything under ``docs/private/``) are
  refused by name — the reads the PM needs do not need the key bytes, so the
  file is never opened rather than opened-then-masked.
* Output is scanned for key-shaped strings and masked before it is returned:
  a second net under the deny rule, not the only one.
* Every call — allowed or refused — appends one line to the audit log on the
  laptop, and ``run`` **refuses when that log is unwritable**: silence is not
  an option.

Deliberately NOT here: any write of any kind on the pod, ``launchctl``
bootstrap/bootout/kickstart, gateway restarts, key rotation, and every
mutating ``evolve-admin`` verb. Those stay the operator's.

Configuration (environment)
---------------------------
``PM_PROBE_HOST``  SSH target. Default ``mini``. Resolved by the operator's
                   own ``~/.ssh/config`` — this server never carries a
                   username, hostname or key path.
``PM_PROBE_LOG``   Audit log path. Default ``~/pm/mini-probe.log``.
``PM_PROBE_SSH``   ssh binary name. Default ``ssh`` (tests override it).

Register / revoke: see internal/meta-system-setup.md § "The PM's read-only pod
probe". To revoke, remove the MCP block — the PM is back to asking.

Stdlib only, on purpose: this runs on the operator's laptop from a checkout,
with no install step and nothing to keep in sync.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

